          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In the standard antenatal high-risk screening model, which of the following is c</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In the standard antenatal high-risk screening model, which of the following is classified as an obstetric risk factor?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Severe anaemia</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Severe anaemia is classified under medical risk factors, not obstetric. Medical risk factors reflect pre-existing maternal illness that complicates pregnancy management.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Antepartum haemorrhage</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Antepartum haemorrhage is one of six obstetric risk factors, alongside previous caesarean section, bad obstetric history, malpresentation at 36 weeks, multiple pregnancy, and grand multiparity.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Chronic kidney disease</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Chronic kidney disease is a medical risk factor. Obstetric risk factors arise directly from the pregnancy or reproductive history, not from underlying maternal disease.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Uncontrolled hypothyroidism</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Hypothyroidism is a medical risk factor that complicates pregnancy. Obstetric risk factors relate to the pregnancy itself — such as malpresentation, APH, or grand multiparity.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct answer is antepartum haemorrhage. Obstetric risk factors arise from the pregnancy or reproductive history itself, while medical risk factors reflect underlying maternal illness."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done! Distinguishing obstetric from medical risk factors is essential for correctly triaging women into facility-based delivery versus community-level monitoring."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
