          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A community health worker visits a family where the grandmother advises discardi</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A community health worker visits a family where the grandmother advises discarding the thick yellow 'first milk' and starting regular breastfeeding only on the third day. Which component of colostrum provides the newborn's primary mucosal immune defence?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Oligosaccharides</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Oligosaccharides in colostrum are prebiotics that establish the neonatal gut microbiome, but they are not antibodies and do not provide direct mucosal immune defence.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Lactoferrin</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Lactoferrin is an antimicrobial protein that inhibits bacterial growth by chelating iron, but it is not an antibody and does not serve as the primary mucosal immune barrier.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Secretory IgA</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Secretory IgA is the primary mucosal immune antibody in colostrum, coating the newborn's gut and respiratory mucosa to block pathogen entry — a key reason colostrum must never be discarded.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Fat-soluble vitamin A</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Vitamin A supports epithelial integrity and general immune function, but it is a micronutrient, not an antibody, and does not serve as the mucosal immune barrier.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The correct answer is secretory IgA. It is the primary mucosal immune antibody in colostrum, providing passive protection to the newborn's gut and respiratory mucosa."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Excellent! Discarding colostrum removes secretory IgA along with lactoferrin, oligosaccharides, and vitamin A — a practice that directly increases neonatal infection risk."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
